title: Attachments: durable results inside a ticket
desc:26
A subagent's output is ephemeral — it lands in the lead agent's context,
gets summarised, and is gone at the next compaction. Wanting a file for it
is right; making it a ticket is not.

A ticket is a directory, so any other file in it is an attachment:
.pidly/7/review.md sits with the work it documents and is committed with it.
Verified harmless — a file attachment does not appear in the tree scan, does
not affect numbering, and is not counted as a ticket.

Attachments must be files, not directories. A directory is read as a child
ticket, so .pidly/7/notes/ becomes a phantom with no info file. Added to 9:
check rejects any directory in a ticket that is neither a number nor c.

Most of the time no attachment is needed. A subagent usually worked on an
existing ticket, so its findings belong in that ticket's body via pidly
edit. Attachments are for results too big for a body, or that are their own
artifact.

Never deleted and kept forever in closed trees, so the same test applies as
for tickets: attach what is worth having tomorrow.

Done: documented in the reference and the design; the check rule is 9's.
